+# Decision: Self-Hosted WebSockets for Change Delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard needs to deliver committed board revisions to up to 800 simultaneous browsers per event. The HLD requires subsecond updates under normal connectivity (Goal: 15s freshness, but subsecond is useful). Three delivery approaches were evaluated:
+
+| Approach | Update Latency | Operational Burden | Recurring Cost | Vendor Coupling |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 5s HTTPS Polling | ~5s avg lag | Low (stateless) | None | None |
+| Self-Hosted WebSockets | <100ms | Medium (connection mgmt, reconnect logic) | None | None |
+| Hosted Realtime (e.g., Pusher, Ably) | <100ms | Low (managed) | ~$200–500/mo at scale | High (API, SDK, limits) |
+
+**Constraints from HLD:**
+- Tenet: "Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted"
+- Tenet: "Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board"
+- Goal: 800 browsers, 20 edits/sec bursts
+- Non-goal: Multi-region active-active for pilot
+- Non-goal: Offline editing
+
+## Options Considered
+
+### Option A: 5-Second HTTPS Polling
+- **Pros:** Simplest implementation; stateless servers; no connection capacity planning; trivial horizontal scaling; works through all proxies/firewalls
+- **Cons:** ~2.5s average latency (half the poll interval); 800 browsers × 12 polls/min = 9,600 requests/min baseline; wasteful during quiet periods; no push semantics means browsers can't distinguish "no change" from "delivery failed"
+
+### Option B: Self-Hosted WebSockets (Chosen)
+- **Pros:** True push — subsecond latency; no per-message cost at scale; browsers distinguish connected vs. disconnected; full control over protocol, backpressure, replay; no vendor lock-in
+- **Cons:** Connection capacity (800 sockets/event, ~10 events = 8k concurrent); reconnect logic required (exponential backoff, catch-up); server-side session state; proxy/firewall traversal considerations; more code to own
+
+### Option C: Hosted Realtime Service
+- **Pros:** Fastest to initial delivery; managed connection scaling; built-in reconnect/presence; SDKs handle edge cases
+- **Cons:** Recurring cost (~$0.40–1.00/million messages + connection fees); provider API coupling (migration = rewrite); rate limits and quotas; data egress through third party; vendor SLA becomes our SLA
+
+## Decision
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets (Option B).**
+
+## Rationale
+
+The choice trades operational complexity for **zero recurring cost** and **zero vendor coupling** — both explicit tenets. At pilot scale (40 markets, ~10 concurrent events, 800 browsers/event), self-hosted WebSocket capacity is well within a modest server footprint (8k connections ≈ 200–400 MB RAM for socket state). The reconnect/catch-up logic is a one-time investment that pays off across all events and avoids perpetual per-message fees.
+
+Polling (A) was rejected because 5s lag violates the "subsecond would be useful" intent and generates 10× more read load during quiet periods. Hosted (C) was rejected because the recurring cost and provider coupling contradict "self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted" — if we can self-host at this scale, we should, and the tenet leans toward owning the stack when feasible.
+
+## Consequences
+
+**Downstream design obligations (cascade to child nodes):**
+
+1. **Change Store** — Must provide a publication outbox (append-only log of revisions) so delivery can replay missed revisions on reconnect.
+2. **Delivery** — Must manage WebSocket lifecycle: accept, authenticate, subscribe to event, publish revisions, handle backpressure, detect stale connections, clean up on disconnect.
+3. **Browser Sync** — Must implement: auto-reconnect with exponential backoff; send last-known revision on reconnect; apply catch-up deltas; surface connection state (connected/reconnecting/disconnected) to UI per "connectivity failures must be visible."
+
+**Capacity planning:** 800 browsers × 10 events = 8k concurrent sockets. Each socket holds ~25 KB state (buffers, subscription). Target: single delivery node handles 10k sockets; horizontal scaling via sticky sessions or shared pub/sub backend.
+
+**Monitoring:** Connection count, message latency (p50/p99), reconnect rate, catch-up duration, failed deliveries.
+
+## Rejected Alternatives Summary
+
+| Alternative | Why Not |
+|---|---|
+| Polling | Latency floor too high; read amplification; no failure visibility |
+| Hosted Realtime | Recurring cost at scale; provider lock-in; tenet permits self-hosted |
+| Server-Sent Events | No bidirectional need, but harder proxy traversal than WS; no advantage over WS here |
+
+## Validation
+
+This decision will be validated when:
+- Delivery LLD specifies session management and replay protocol
+- Browser-sync LLD specifies reconnect/catch-up behavior
+- Load test: 8k sockets, 20 edits/sec burst, <100ms p99 delivery latency
+- Chaos test: kill delivery node, verify browsers reconnect and catch up within 5s
